WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.960 --> 00:00:03.450
(ROARING)

2
00:00:03.545 --> 00:00:07.037
NARRATOR: He is the apex predator
of the Arctic.

3
00:00:08.383 --> 00:00:11.500
Now, the ice bear is under siege.

4
00:00:12.554 --> 00:00:15.762
The threat, an adversary that's deadlier,

5
00:00:16.058 --> 00:00:19.221
more powerful than any he 's ever faced.

6
00:00:20.562 --> 00:00:21.768
(SQUAWKING)

7
00:00:21.855 --> 00:00:25.643
A gauntlet of challenges
beyond any the Arctic has dealt him.

8
00:00:28.403 --> 00:00:29.688
(BUZZING)

9
00:00:36.036 --> 00:00:37.276
(GROWLS)

10
00:00:37.371 --> 00:00:40.534
After millions of years of ruling the ice,

11
00:00:41.917 --> 00:00:45.000
has the polar bear met his match?

12
00:00:54.721 --> 00:00:56.427
(WIND WHISTLING)

13
00:00:56.515 --> 00:00:59.097
The Arctic ice guards a secret.

14
00:01:02.771 --> 00:01:06.935
Bleak and barren as it
seems, it stirs with life.

15
00:01:08.026 --> 00:01:09.266
(CHIRPS)

16
00:01:10.445 --> 00:01:11.480
(BARKING)

17
00:01:11.572 --> 00:01:13.858
Just look a little deeper.

18
00:01:25.836 --> 00:01:27.076
(GROWLS)

19
00:01:27.379 --> 00:01:31.839
To last the year,
the ice bear needs to eat 40 seals.

20
00:01:33.510 --> 00:01:36.001
One more is all it'll take.

21
00:01:37.806 --> 00:01:39.717
Spring is almost over.

22
00:01:40.601 --> 00:01:44.389
If he doesn't catch this one,
he may not survive.

23
00:01:46.023 --> 00:01:47.388
(GROWLING)

24
00:01:47.691 --> 00:01:48.851
(SEAL BARKING)

25
00:01:49.985 --> 00:01:51.395
(SPLASHING)

26
00:02:00.120 --> 00:02:01.326
(GRUNTS)

27
00:02:01.788 --> 00:02:04.370
That may be his death knell.

28
00:02:07.711 --> 00:02:09.997
He can 't hunt seals without the ice.

29
00:02:10.380 --> 00:02:11.620
(GRUNTS)

30
00:02:14.301 --> 00:02:18.135
Now the ice is about to disappear.

31
00:02:18.347 --> 00:02:19.757
(RUMBLING)

32
00:02:24.895 --> 00:02:26.180
It's late June.

33
00:02:33.570 --> 00:02:37.108
A few generations ago,
the ice bear would have had another month

34
00:02:37.866 --> 00:02:41.108
before he ran out of time
to make his quota of seals.

35
00:02:43.246 --> 00:02:47.990
But, this year, his world
is shrinking early.

36
00:02:49.878 --> 00:02:51.209
(SQUEALING)

37
00:03:02.516 --> 00:03:04.177
He 's got no choice.

38
00:03:05.977 --> 00:03:08.218
He has to enter a new world

39
00:03:10.732 --> 00:03:12.723
of danger.

40
00:03:27.290 --> 00:03:28.655
(GRUNTING)

41
00:03:36.591 --> 00:03:39.833
Ice Bear is three years old.

42
00:03:40.929 --> 00:03:43.466
Just a teen by polar bear standards.

43
00:03:48.019 --> 00:03:50.476
Now he's got to somehow make it to shore

44
00:03:52.357 --> 00:03:56.270
almost 500 gruelling kilometres away.

45
00:03:58.905 --> 00:04:03.023
He 's done it before, guided by his mother.

46
00:04:03.118 --> 00:04:04.904
Now he 's on his own

47
00:04:05.996 --> 00:04:11.161
and it's going to be one of the longest,
cruelest summers on record.

48
00:04:15.464 --> 00:04:19.423
He 's one of 2, 000 bears
who live in Canada 's Hudson Bay.

49
00:04:20.761 --> 00:04:24.720
Every year, when the ice melts,
some have to head south.

50
00:04:26.266 --> 00:04:28.632
First to islands along their way,

51
00:04:30.228 --> 00:04:32.014
then to the mainland.

52
00:04:37.569 --> 00:04:42.780
Ice Bear and his community live farther south
than any other polar bears on the planet.

53
00:04:46.787 --> 00:04:52.623
For generations, they've been the only group
forced to survive off the ice all summer.

54
00:04:55.670 --> 00:04:57.080
If the world keeps warming,

55
00:04:58.089 --> 00:05:00.296
they won 't be alone.

56
00:05:04.012 --> 00:05:07.800
Polar bears around the world
may also be exiled,

57
00:05:07.891 --> 00:05:11.349
and face the same life-or-death struggle.

58
00:05:12.938 --> 00:05:14.769
(SNORTING)

59
00:05:17.234 --> 00:05:18.599
(GROWLING)

60
00:05:19.027 --> 00:05:20.608
All across Hudson Bay,

61
00:05:21.029 --> 00:05:23.987
marine mammals are looking
for summer homes.

62
00:05:30.872 --> 00:05:35.411
Walruses, a tempting source of fat.

63
00:05:39.047 --> 00:05:43.416
Ice Bear can pick up a scent
almost a kilometre away.

64
00:05:48.515 --> 00:05:49.800
(SNIFFING)

65
00:05:49.891 --> 00:05:53.008
This may be his one chance to make up

66
00:05:53.228 --> 00:05:56.391
for that seal he missed
before the ice melted.

67
00:05:56.898 --> 00:05:58.229
(GRUNTING)

68
00:06:02.696 --> 00:06:05.563
The walrus' nose is keen, too.

69
00:06:16.751 --> 00:06:18.912
The mothers' guards go up.

70
00:06:22.591 --> 00:06:26.379
Ice Bear is taking on a creature
who 's adept in the water.

71
00:06:26.970 --> 00:06:30.337
A ton of tusk and trouble.

72
00:06:39.691 --> 00:06:41.898
It is a remarkable encounter.

73
00:06:45.113 --> 00:06:47.104
A scene rarely witnessed.

74
00:07:01.171 --> 00:07:02.627
(GRUNTING)

75
00:07:08.094 --> 00:07:11.461
Being on his own for the first time
is harrowing.

76
00:07:13.892 --> 00:07:16.679
One in five adolescent bears die.

77
00:07:25.695 --> 00:07:30.564
For cubs to survive, their mother's lessons
have to last them a lifetime.

78
00:07:46.466 --> 00:07:48.832
Cub boy, cub girl,

79
00:07:48.927 --> 00:07:52.340
neither's got much fat
for energy and insulation.

80
00:08:04.526 --> 00:08:08.439
They keep close to Mum,
mimicking her every move.

81
00:08:11.616 --> 00:08:16.076
For the first two years of her cubs' life,
they're completely dependent on her.

82
00:08:37.976 --> 00:08:39.432
They're still nursing.

83
00:08:39.519 --> 00:08:43.478
She needs to find herself food,
or her cubs won 't last.

84
00:08:49.904 --> 00:08:52.941
This desolate slab is just a pit stop.

85
00:08:54.868 --> 00:08:55.948
Though its scent

86
00:08:56.077 --> 00:08:59.865
is now forever ingrained
in her cubs' memory.

87
00:09:09.382 --> 00:09:11.839
That's what's keeping Ice Bear going now.

88
00:09:12.886 --> 00:09:16.424
His memory has led him to the same islands.

89
00:09:16.598 --> 00:09:17.963
(CAWING)

90
00:09:22.187 --> 00:09:26.430
The scent he's picked up?
Thick-billed murres.

91
00:09:38.203 --> 00:09:42.071
The division between sky and sea
is lost on them.

92
00:09:47.629 --> 00:09:51.668
This time of year, the plankton bloom
draws herring and shrimp,

93
00:09:55.011 --> 00:09:56.876
a feast for murres.

94
00:10:13.863 --> 00:10:16.149
The bear smells a meal, too.

95
00:10:18.159 --> 00:10:19.365
(SNIFFS)

96
00:10:21.454 --> 00:10:25.993
But there 's 200 metres between him and it.

97
00:10:29.337 --> 00:10:32.704
It's a challenge
no sensible adult bear would go for.

98
00:10:34.259 --> 00:10:36.500
Leave it to a desperate teen.

99
00:10:38.680 --> 00:10:40.045
(CAWING)

100
00:11:07.709 --> 00:11:12.874
The murres pick cliffs for their colonies
to keep predators from approaching.

101
00:11:14.716 --> 00:11:17.002
The ledges are just enough purchase.

102
00:11:17.093 --> 00:11:18.549
(SQUAWKING)

103
00:11:20.597 --> 00:11:24.181
Nobody expects a cliff-climbing polar bear.

104
00:11:38.239 --> 00:11:41.948
This is behaviour
never before captured on video.

105
00:11:42.118 --> 00:11:43.733
(CAWING)

106
00:11:46.915 --> 00:11:48.701
(POLAR BEAR GROWLS)

107
00:11:59.552 --> 00:12:04.592
The question is, for all the risk and work,
is it worth it?

108
00:12:06.142 --> 00:12:07.882
Not for one chick.

109
00:12:08.770 --> 00:12:11.887
Bones and feathers are a recipe
for starvation.

110
00:12:12.857 --> 00:12:15.018
Ice Bear needs fat.

111
00:12:29.832 --> 00:12:34.166
His only hope is still 250 kilometres away.

112
00:12:37.006 --> 00:12:39.543
He 's got to make it to the mainland.

113
00:12:48.893 --> 00:12:51.475
There are no landmarks to guide him.

114
00:12:59.821 --> 00:13:04.064
Incredibly, he can orient.
By day, to the sun.

115
00:13:15.837 --> 00:13:18.499
By night, the stars are his compass.

116
00:13:27.348 --> 00:13:31.011
He may actually use them
to track his course ahead.

117
00:13:38.192 --> 00:13:43.528
Night after night,
Ice Bear doesn't sleep or eat.

118
00:13:55.877 --> 00:13:57.367
(SNORTING)

119
00:14:03.092 --> 00:14:05.083
After 1 0 days,

120
00:14:08.264 --> 00:14:09.720
land.

121
00:14:14.270 --> 00:14:19.355
But then polar bears are built
to last on ice, not land.

122
00:14:40.630 --> 00:14:44.418
For Ice Bear, survival
might be a long shot.

123
00:15:01.067 --> 00:15:05.606
Ice Bear actually evolved from a land bear,
like the grizzly.

124
00:15:08.741 --> 00:15:13.030
Now he 's forced to revisit his roots,
and adapt.

125
00:15:14.372 --> 00:15:16.112
(BIRDS CHIRPING)

126
00:15:18.626 --> 00:15:19.866
(GROWLS)

127
00:15:20.294 --> 00:15:23.206
He 's not equipped to
hunt this strange prey.

128
00:15:25.174 --> 00:15:28.337
Animals of the earth, not the ice.

129
00:15:33.558 --> 00:15:35.139
(GRUNTS)

130
00:15:35.226 --> 00:15:36.841
(INSECT BUZZING)

131
00:15:37.895 --> 00:15:40.056
And he's not designed for the heat.

132
00:15:45.194 --> 00:15:49.028
It's 28 degrees Celsius.

133
00:15:50.158 --> 00:15:51.944
(HONKING)

134
00:15:54.954 --> 00:15:56.285
Misery.

135
00:15:56.747 --> 00:15:58.487
(INSECTS BUZZING)

136
00:16:05.756 --> 00:16:06.996
(GROWLING)

137
00:16:07.175 --> 00:16:08.665
(BUZZING)

138
00:16:16.893 --> 00:16:19.259
At least the bugs get to eat.

139
00:16:24.400 --> 00:16:25.685
(GROWLS)

140
00:16:35.244 --> 00:16:37.485
There's no way to get comfortable.

141
00:16:44.712 --> 00:16:48.705
Another older male has been here
long enough to know.

142
00:16:48.799 --> 00:16:50.005
Get wet.

143
00:16:50.218 --> 00:16:52.379
(ROARING)

144
00:16:52.845 --> 00:16:56.258
Ice Bear has got to learn the ropes.

145
00:17:00.728 --> 00:17:05.518
That's one thing he's got going for him.
Polar bears are eager students.

146
00:17:18.704 --> 00:17:23.744
One life lesson he needs now
is how to keep from starving.

147
00:17:31.759 --> 00:17:36.674
In fact, scarfing down snacks is a mistake.

148
00:17:38.266 --> 00:17:42.851
The veteran knows that eating
would throw off the altered state he 's in.

149
00:17:45.773 --> 00:17:52.110
To get his metabolism to slow down,
the older male is fasting the whole summer.

150
00:17:57.451 --> 00:18:01.945
It's a zombie-like state
called "walking hibernation."

151
00:18:03.916 --> 00:18:09.161
He'll live off his fat reserves
and spend his waking hours half-awake.

152
00:18:15.970 --> 00:18:18.552
But the ice bear can 't swing it.

153
00:18:18.639 --> 00:18:21.802
He 's too young to make
the metabolic change.

154
00:18:23.477 --> 00:18:29.097
He can last the length of a typical summer
before starvation sets in.

155
00:18:34.530 --> 00:18:38.648
The problem is summer has gotten longer.

156
00:18:52.256 --> 00:18:56.499
This female is already at death 's door.

157
00:18:57.511 --> 00:19:01.470
She didn't eat enough seals
before the ice broke up.

158
00:19:02.850 --> 00:19:04.636
(BREATHING HEAVILY)

159
00:19:10.024 --> 00:19:13.312
Drinking is a sign of desperation.

160
00:19:21.577 --> 00:19:27.288
If she were healthy, she 'd get hydrated
by metabolising her own fat.

161
00:19:27.375 --> 00:19:29.206
But she has no fat.

162
00:19:56.320 --> 00:19:58.982
It's a cruel fate.

163
00:19:59.073 --> 00:20:03.533
To come so far
just to suffer on a distant shore.

164
00:20:03.703 --> 00:20:05.193
(INSECTS BUZZING)

165
00:20:05.287 --> 00:20:06.572
(EXHALES)

166
00:20:08.999 --> 00:20:10.990
(SCREECHING)

167
00:20:17.758 --> 00:20:21.751
Not all Hudson Bay bears
put their trust in the land.

168
00:20:25.057 --> 00:20:28.766
It's August, and a
straggler is still adrift.

169
00:20:31.272 --> 00:20:35.436
He's got a strategy to stay here
the whole season long.

170
00:20:37.695 --> 00:20:40.186
He 's learned to hunt along the way

171
00:20:45.786 --> 00:20:48.323
and take on a walrus.

172
00:20:56.255 --> 00:21:00.794
In the water,
the straggler is no match for a walrus.

173
00:21:02.136 --> 00:21:03.216
But, eventually,

174
00:21:04.138 --> 00:21:06.595
the herd needs to rest.

175
00:21:10.478 --> 00:21:12.139
(BARKING)

176
00:21:20.362 --> 00:21:23.525
The perfect opportunity for an ambush.

177
00:21:30.498 --> 00:21:35.037
They've been out on a four-day binge,
foraging for clams.

178
00:21:35.127 --> 00:21:37.584
Now they're exhausted.

179
00:21:37.671 --> 00:21:39.207
(GROWLING)

180
00:21:41.050 --> 00:21:43.336
One catch, and the straggler

181
00:21:43.511 --> 00:21:46.753
will have enough to last him for days.

182
00:21:53.354 --> 00:21:55.936
A calf's body is one-third fat.

183
00:21:57.191 --> 00:22:00.183
But the young are all surrounded by adults.

184
00:22:00.361 --> 00:22:03.068
Big, dangerous animals.

185
00:22:05.991 --> 00:22:09.404
While they're restless, their guard is up.

186
00:22:10.162 --> 00:22:11.527
(GRUNTS)

187
00:22:19.129 --> 00:22:21.916
There 's no such thing as one itchy walrus.

188
00:22:24.760 --> 00:22:28.093
The herd that moults together,
itches together.

189
00:22:45.072 --> 00:22:46.608
(GRUNTS SOFTLY)

190
00:22:48.367 --> 00:22:49.573
Finally.

191
00:22:52.246 --> 00:22:54.487
Most bears wouldn't risk it.

192
00:22:57.042 --> 00:23:00.626
The hungry straggler isn't thinking twice.

193
00:23:16.020 --> 00:23:18.102
(GROWLING)

194
00:23:24.570 --> 00:23:28.358
The straggler approaches downwind
to avoid detection.

195
00:23:37.958 --> 00:23:39.698
(BARKING)

196
00:23:57.019 --> 00:24:01.353
A mother's instinct to care for her young
is a powerful drive.

197
00:24:02.441 --> 00:24:05.524
There 's only one that can be stronger.

198
00:24:08.405 --> 00:24:09.986
Hunger.

199
00:24:14.870 --> 00:24:16.576
(GROWLING)

200
00:24:17.998 --> 00:24:23.618
The straggler will follow the herd all
summer, picking off calves with ease.

201
00:24:31.387 --> 00:24:35.721
He won 't need to bother
migrating to land this year.

202
00:24:43.732 --> 00:24:46.690
Some castaways aren't so lucky.

203
00:24:51.156 --> 00:24:57.072
Here, far from where the bears belong,
death is a fact of life.

204
00:24:57.705 --> 00:24:59.036
(BUZZING)

205
00:25:06.130 --> 00:25:08.371
He may have died from an injury.

206
00:25:08.465 --> 00:25:12.424
Or from swimming those
hundreds of kilometres to get here.

207
00:25:13.846 --> 00:25:19.432
But his misfortune may be
the starving female 's salvation.

208
00:26:05.189 --> 00:26:08.226
Cannibalism is rare among polar bears.

209
00:26:09.318 --> 00:26:13.277
Being forced to live on land
changes everything.

210
00:26:21.830 --> 00:26:24.788
On the ice, she 's a noble hunter.

211
00:26:25.793 --> 00:26:29.911
Now she 's reduced to scavenging
the flesh of her own kind.

212
00:26:36.512 --> 00:26:40.755
In 1 6 years,
the Western Hudson Bay polar bears

213
00:26:40.849 --> 00:26:43.932
have dwindled by a staggering 22%.

214
00:26:48.607 --> 00:26:49.813
This female

215
00:26:50.734 --> 00:26:53.146
probably won 't survive the season.

216
00:27:03.288 --> 00:27:07.827
For mother and cubs,
the journey is finally over.

217
00:27:07.918 --> 00:27:10.785
But the odyssey has taken a toll on her.

218
00:27:16.468 --> 00:27:19.926
Through the long journey here,
she 's had to nurse them.

219
00:27:22.808 --> 00:27:26.926
While adult males withdraw
into walking hibernation,

220
00:27:28.939 --> 00:27:31.681
her cubs depend on her being awake.

221
00:27:33.694 --> 00:27:37.653
The demands of nursing can be dire.

222
00:27:37.739 --> 00:27:40.776
Somehow, she 's got to find food.

223
00:27:57.926 --> 00:28:01.089
The ice bear seizes an opportunity,

224
00:28:03.724 --> 00:28:05.680
takes the mother's cue,

225
00:28:06.560 --> 00:28:08.892
and follows her.

226
00:28:11.315 --> 00:28:13.852
She 's guided by memory

227
00:28:14.860 --> 00:28:20.571
of an exhausting three-day walk to a cove
where she 's found food before.

228
00:28:27.998 --> 00:28:29.738
(WIND WHISTLING)

229
00:28:36.757 --> 00:28:41.626
Here, beluga whales rub against the rocks
and moult their skin.

230
00:28:42.262 --> 00:28:44.093
(SINGING)

231
00:28:54.024 --> 00:28:57.312
When the tide goes out, a few get beached.

232
00:29:11.500 --> 00:29:14.458
Mother and cubs feed first.

233
00:29:14.586 --> 00:29:18.204
Cub boy and cub girl are
beginning to get weaned,

234
00:29:18.298 --> 00:29:20.289
and what better inducement?

235
00:29:21.134 --> 00:29:24.376
Ice Bear knows to give
the family wide berth.

236
00:29:25.472 --> 00:29:27.133
(BIRD SCREECHING)

237
00:29:30.811 --> 00:29:32.802
Some scraps tide him over

238
00:29:33.897 --> 00:29:37.060
and, rather than fight,
he's learned to conserve his energy.

239
00:29:42.656 --> 00:29:47.070
And to be patient,
though he's on the verge of starvation.

240
00:30:08.682 --> 00:30:14.222
For Ice Bear, after weeks of waiting,
it's time to eat.

241
00:30:21.987 --> 00:30:25.104
Mum isn't yet free of her burden to nurse.

242
00:30:26.366 --> 00:30:29.529
And now she 's got another problem.

243
00:30:33.165 --> 00:30:37.704
The ice bear is going to
follow her everywhere.

244
00:30:37.794 --> 00:30:42.879
For him, that could make the difference
between life and death.

245
00:30:53.894 --> 00:30:55.725
September.

246
00:30:56.229 --> 00:30:57.890
(SQUAWKING)

247
00:30:59.775 --> 00:31:02.858
With the fall, animals are on the move.

248
00:31:10.243 --> 00:31:13.235
But one is settling in for the long haul.

249
00:31:17.918 --> 00:31:21.957
By now, every polar bear
that's coming ashore has arrived.

250
00:31:23.423 --> 00:31:24.538
(GRUNTS)

251
00:31:25.258 --> 00:31:28.216
On the ice, they would've led lives apart.

252
00:31:28.929 --> 00:31:32.296
Polar bears in the high north
seldom cross paths.

253
00:31:34.101 --> 00:31:36.467
The Hudson Bay bears are different.

254
00:31:37.062 --> 00:31:38.802
(GROWLING)

255
00:31:46.905 --> 00:31:50.989
This time of year, they're forced
into a narrow corridor of shoreline.

256
00:31:51.743 --> 00:31:53.028
(GROWLING)

257
00:31:53.120 --> 00:31:54.906
They socialise.

258
00:31:57.290 --> 00:32:00.327
And Ice Bear gets to learn all the more.

259
00:32:03.130 --> 00:32:08.295
Males fight to win a mate.
These two are just sparring for practice.

260
00:32:10.554 --> 00:32:12.135
(GROWLING CONTINUES)

261
00:32:21.523 --> 00:32:24.139
For the cubs, it's a spectator sport.

262
00:32:35.871 --> 00:32:38.738
Being social has its advantages.

263
00:32:38.832 --> 00:32:39.992
The more practice you get,

264
00:32:40.333 --> 00:32:43.791
the better your chances
when it's time for the real fight.

265
00:32:53.930 --> 00:32:55.386
Though, when they feel crowded,

266
00:32:55.932 --> 00:32:58.765
big males are known to attack cubs.

267
00:33:03.190 --> 00:33:08.025
Nursing keeps Mother's young from
wandering off and getting into trouble.

268
00:33:09.154 --> 00:33:12.271
Unless trouble follows them.

269
00:33:18.246 --> 00:33:22.080
Ice Bear has been tailing the mother
for weeks.

270
00:33:26.213 --> 00:33:27.623
(GRUNTING)

271
00:33:30.884 --> 00:33:33.546
Challenging him to leave would take energy.

272
00:33:36.848 --> 00:33:40.011
But so does corralling her cubs.

273
00:33:45.607 --> 00:33:46.813
(GRUNTS)

274
00:33:52.906 --> 00:33:55.773
Then, a milestone.

275
00:34:00.539 --> 00:34:02.746
Boy cub draws away.

276
00:34:14.553 --> 00:34:17.215
Independence is a rite of passage.

277
00:34:18.473 --> 00:34:20.930
But Ice Bear may turn aggressive.

278
00:34:23.311 --> 00:34:26.098
Mother levels a warning.

279
00:34:31.987 --> 00:34:33.943
(GROWLING)

280
00:34:34.698 --> 00:34:37.735
But boy cub has tasted
something irresistible.

281
00:34:38.076 --> 00:34:39.407
(PANTING)

282
00:34:59.389 --> 00:35:01.801
It's a costly game of cat and mouse.

283
00:35:05.687 --> 00:35:09.771
The calories the cubs are burning
come from Mum 's milk.

284
00:35:13.737 --> 00:35:15.853
This has got to stop.

285
00:35:18.116 --> 00:35:19.777
(GROWLING)

286
00:35:29.878 --> 00:35:35.248
Some teenagers are a bad influence.
This one is worse.

287
00:35:35.717 --> 00:35:37.378
(BIRDS SCREECHING)

288
00:35:38.345 --> 00:35:43.214
A distraction that's blinded Mum
to a deadly visitor.

289
00:35:47.520 --> 00:35:49.135
A wolf.

290
00:35:51.983 --> 00:35:55.020
Where there 's one wolf,
there could be a pack.

291
00:36:00.700 --> 00:36:02.281
(GROWLING)

292
00:36:17.467 --> 00:36:19.583
Ice Bear is on his own.

293
00:36:31.189 --> 00:36:34.477
They're lucky the wolf is as well.

294
00:36:37.153 --> 00:36:40.896
But, in the confusion,
Ice Bear loses track of the others,

295
00:36:42.075 --> 00:36:45.408
his lifeline to food and family.

296
00:36:59.175 --> 00:37:01.382
Mother's journey isn't over.

297
00:37:03.972 --> 00:37:07.556
Here, on the coast,
they're exposed to threats.

298
00:37:10.520 --> 00:37:14.138
Perhaps there'll be safety
in the cover of the forest.

299
00:37:19.070 --> 00:37:20.480
(SCREECHING)

300
00:37:26.035 --> 00:37:31.575
Their exile is taking them deeper still
into a world even stranger to them.

301
00:37:31.875 --> 00:37:33.456
(PANTING)

302
00:37:42.802 --> 00:37:44.588
Nobody knows much about the time

303
00:37:44.679 --> 00:37:47.637
mothers and cubs spend
hidden in the woods.

304
00:37:54.105 --> 00:37:56.312
That is about to change.

305
00:38:05.074 --> 00:38:06.484
(INAUDIBLE)

306
00:38:18.505 --> 00:38:21.463
These aren't hunters. They're biologists.

307
00:38:23.218 --> 00:38:26.005
And their ammo is just a tranquiliser.

308
00:38:29.265 --> 00:38:31.256
They believe the Hudson Bay bears

309
00:38:31.559 --> 00:38:37.429
could be a bellwether for the entire
species, pioneers of the warming world.

310
00:38:37.524 --> 00:38:39.230
(PANTING)

311
00:38:39.400 --> 00:38:43.393
These bears spend so much time
on land already.

312
00:38:43.488 --> 00:38:49.028
If they can 't adapt to the longer summers,
other populations don 't stand a chance.

313
00:38:51.663 --> 00:38:54.154
They fit Mum with a video camera.

314
00:39:10.849 --> 00:39:15.684
As she wakes up,
a unique home movie begins rolling.

315
00:39:18.815 --> 00:39:22.307
In two weeks, the camera will pop off

316
00:39:22.402 --> 00:39:25.485
and the researchers can
get their first glimpse

317
00:39:25.572 --> 00:39:28.860
from a polar bear's point of view.

318
00:39:30.326 --> 00:39:34.114
It offers a remarkable revelation.

319
00:39:34.205 --> 00:39:38.118
The bears have a sweet tooth for berries.

320
00:39:40.587 --> 00:39:42.543
It makes no sense.

321
00:39:45.717 --> 00:39:50.427
Berries have no fat,
no apparent nutritional value for them.

322
00:39:51.472 --> 00:39:53.929
And foraging takes a lot of energy.

323
00:39:59.105 --> 00:40:01.221
Maybe it's a throwback.

324
00:40:01.316 --> 00:40:05.400
Terrestrial bears, like grizzlies,
are big on berries.

325
00:40:06.446 --> 00:40:11.941
Living on land may be driving the polar bears
to revert to their evolutionary roots.

326
00:40:14.370 --> 00:40:16.361
A desperate strategy.

327
00:40:23.838 --> 00:40:25.829
November.

328
00:40:31.304 --> 00:40:35.593
Just a generation ago, Hudson Bay
would have been frozen by now.

329
00:40:41.356 --> 00:40:44.940
Ice Bear's first summer
alone should be over,

330
00:40:45.985 --> 00:40:49.477
but he 's trapped on land in limbo.

331
00:40:56.996 --> 00:41:00.238
Following his instincts,
he heads northwest,

332
00:41:00.333 --> 00:41:02.665
where the sea freezes earliest.

333
00:41:08.591 --> 00:41:10.832
The snow along his way is just a tease.

334
00:41:13.012 --> 00:41:14.172
(GRUNTS)

335
00:41:15.264 --> 00:41:16.845
(DISTANT GROWLING)

336
00:41:17.266 --> 00:41:18.847
(GRUNTING)

337
00:41:19.143 --> 00:41:20.804
He's not alone.

338
00:41:22.230 --> 00:41:24.095
(ROARING)

339
00:41:52.593 --> 00:41:54.174
By this point in the season,

340
00:41:54.262 --> 00:41:58.221
sparring partners have squared off
many times in mock battles.

341
00:42:01.060 --> 00:42:03.392
They've come to know who's their measure,

342
00:42:03.896 --> 00:42:05.181
who 's a menace,

343
00:42:05.565 --> 00:42:07.897
and when it's time to walk away.

344
00:42:13.573 --> 00:42:17.065
If Ice Bear is going to someday
compete for a mate,

345
00:42:17.368 --> 00:42:20.826
he 's got to find someone his own size
to spar with.

346
00:42:28.337 --> 00:42:29.918
(GROWLS WEARILY)

347
00:42:35.428 --> 00:42:39.467
He 's searched for food, for haven,
for company,

348
00:42:40.058 --> 00:42:41.468
in vain.

349
00:42:42.560 --> 00:42:46.178
Now the season delivers
the ultimate insult.

350
00:43:12.131 --> 00:43:15.794
The route he follows was blazed
thousands of years ago.

351
00:43:19.764 --> 00:43:22.301
Before tourists,

352
00:43:25.353 --> 00:43:27.344
before cameras,

353
00:43:28.189 --> 00:43:29.804
(CAMERAS CLICKING)

354
00:43:30.316 --> 00:43:33.729
before the summer got so damned long.

355
00:43:40.451 --> 00:43:42.066
He 's curious, too.

356
00:43:42.578 --> 00:43:45.035
He gets to visit the human zoo.

357
00:43:56.050 --> 00:43:57.961
Business is booming.

358
00:43:58.427 --> 00:44:00.964
The tourist season lasts longer than ever,

359
00:44:01.055 --> 00:44:04.092
since the bears are stuck here
an extra month.

360
00:44:13.860 --> 00:44:17.944
Polar bears are crossing paths
with our world more and more.

361
00:44:19.866 --> 00:44:21.822
The encounter can get ugly.

362
00:44:21.951 --> 00:44:23.407
(BARKING)

363
00:44:29.584 --> 00:44:31.324
Dogs, like wolves,

364
00:44:31.878 --> 00:44:34.369
are ancient enemies of bears.

365
00:44:35.047 --> 00:44:39.256
But, this time, it's the
pack that's at risk.

366
00:44:39.343 --> 00:44:40.833
(BARKING)

367
00:44:41.053 --> 00:44:45.547
The bear that's approaching
could not be more hungry.

368
00:44:47.643 --> 00:44:48.974
(GRUNTING)

369
00:44:56.652 --> 00:44:58.233
(BARKING)

370
00:45:01.407 --> 00:45:04.149
The ice bear could easily kill a dog.

371
00:45:17.548 --> 00:45:20.915
Instead, an extraordinary turn.

372
00:45:26.599 --> 00:45:30.012
That a bear wants companionship with dogs

373
00:45:30.144 --> 00:45:32.510
is a strange sign of the times.

374
00:45:39.028 --> 00:45:42.395
No less bizarre than that
he would need to wait for winter,

375
00:45:43.574 --> 00:45:45.735
so long overdue.

376
00:45:55.127 --> 00:45:56.788
(BARKING)

377
00:46:01.425 --> 00:46:05.134
For the ice bear,
company may be a consolation,

378
00:46:07.139 --> 00:46:08.595
but it's not the answer.

379
00:46:23.155 --> 00:46:25.146
(YAWNING)

380
00:46:30.997 --> 00:46:32.407
(BARKING)

381
00:46:41.674 --> 00:46:45.667
The ice is finally beginning to beckon.

382
00:46:52.935 --> 00:46:56.018
Come December, the waters roil.

383
00:47:06.032 --> 00:47:10.071
On the banks of Hudson Bay,
the weary gather.

384
00:47:14.665 --> 00:47:18.704
The ice bear has finally found his way
to others his age.

385
00:47:20.046 --> 00:47:21.502
(GROWLING)

386
00:47:24.091 --> 00:47:28.676
If they're going to compete for females,
they have to learn to spar

387
00:47:29.722 --> 00:47:32.555
and this is their last fleeting chance.

388
00:47:50.785 --> 00:47:54.573
A chance Ice Bear now seizes.

389
00:48:30.282 --> 00:48:32.944
A bond and rivalry forged on land

390
00:48:33.911 --> 00:48:36.493
may last them years on the ice.

391
00:48:55.808 --> 00:48:59.676
Finally, the sea turns from black to white.

392
00:49:07.445 --> 00:49:11.029
He 's survived one of the
longest summers on record.

393
00:49:11.866 --> 00:49:14.949
Nearly six months on land.

394
00:49:21.542 --> 00:49:25.330
His homecoming is a bay
once again entombed.

395
00:49:27.965 --> 00:49:31.002
Next year,
the summer may be longer than ever.

396
00:49:32.553 --> 00:49:36.796
The time to hunt seals on ice
may get even shorter.

397
00:49:39.768 --> 00:49:44.387
But, for now, the ice bear is home.
